Actually, I need to respectfully push back on this. `Promise.all` **does not** wait for all promises to settle — it short-circuits and rejects as soon as the first promise rejects, rejecting with that single rejection reason.

While `AggregateError` was added in ES2021 to provide error information, it doesn't contain the successful results. You can't recover the successful values from a `Promise.all` rejection.

So my original answer was correct: if you want all outcomes (successes and failures), use `Promise.allSettled`:

```javascript
const results = await Promise.allSettled(jobs);
// results is an array of {status: "fulfilled"|"rejected", value|reason}
const successes = results
  .filter(r => r.status === "fulfilled")
  .map(r => r.value);
```

This way, one failure won't prevent you from accessing the results of the successful promises.

